speechtext:
I had better not comment on that. You are my brothers and my sisters.
I rise on a point of order with respect to two items of House business on which there have been consultations. At this point in time, I think you will find, Mr. Speaker, that if you were to ask for unanimous consent to two orders, which 1 would bring forward at this time, you would find that there would be a disposition to pass them by unanimous consent. The first order relates to committee on acid rain and reads as follows:
That a Special Committee consisting of Messrs. Blaikie, Caccia, Darling, Ferland, Gurbin, Schelienberg and St-Julien be appointed to act as a Special Committee on Acid Rain;
That the Committee hold hearings to review all aspects of acid rain;
That the evidence adduced by the Sub-Committee on Acid Rain of the Standing Committee on Fisheries and Forestry in the First Session of the present Parliament be deemed to have been referred to the Committee;
That the Committee have all the powers provided to standing committees pursuant to Standing Order 69(8);
That the provisions of sections (4) and (9) of Standing Order 69 of the House of Commons should be suspended, unless otherwise agreed to by this Committee.
